test version 27 May 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:On the Fallacy of Global Token Perplexity in Spoken Language Model Evaluation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Jeff Chan-Jan Sju , cite7†Liang-Hsuan Tseng , cite8†Yi-Cheng Lin , cite9†Yen-Chun Kuo , cite10†Ju-Chieh Chou , cite11†Kai-Wei Chang , cite12†Hung-yi Lee , cite13†Carlos Busso L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled On the Fallacy of Global Token Perplexity in Spoken Language Model Evaluation, by Jeff Chan-Jan Sju and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Generative spoken language models pretrained on large-scale raw audio can continue a speech prompt with appropriate content while preserving attributes like speaker and emotion, serving as foundation models for spoken dialogue. In prior literature, these models are often evaluated using ``global token perplexity'', which directly applies the text perplexity formulation to speech tokens.\nL17: However, this practice overlooks fundamental differences between speech and text modalities, possibly leading to an underestimation of the speech characteristics. In this work, we propose a variety of likelihood- and generative-based evaluation methods that serve in place of naive global token perplexity. We demonstrate that the proposed evaluations more faithfully reflect perceived generation quality, as evidenced by stronger correlations with human-rated mean opinion scores (MOS).\nL18: When assessed under the new metrics, the relative performance landscape of spoken language models is reshaped, revealing a significantly reduced gap between the best-performing model and the human topline. Together, these results suggest that appropriate evaluation is critical for accurately assessing progress in spoken language modeling.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2601.06329 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2601.06329v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.06329†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Chan-Jan Hsu [cite19†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Fri, 9 Jan 2026 22:01:56 UTC (1,253 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Wed, 27 May 2026 06:16:34 UTC (1,244 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled On the Fallacy of Global Token Perplexity in Spoken Language Model Evaluation, by Jeff Chan-Jan Sju and 7 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite14†View PDF L36:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite20†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L45: \nL46: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2026-01 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite27†cs L51: cite28†cs.AI L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite31†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite32†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite33†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (cite34†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL87: \nL88: scite Smart Citations (cite35†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL89: \nL90: [Input] Code, Data, Med